<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Tirigan</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 2120 BCE</span></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Tirigan was the last Gutian king listed in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sumerian-king-list">Sumerian King List</a>, said to have reigned for only forty days. Around 2119 BCE <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/utu-hengal">Utu-hengal</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uruk">Uruk</a> defeated his army; Tirigan fled to the town of Dubrum, where he was seized and handed over, ending Gutian rule in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesopotamia">Mesopotamia</a>. The victory is recorded in Utu-hengal's own inscription.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/tirigan.jpg" alt="Tirigan" width="320" height="527" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: François Thureau-Dangin (3 January 1872 in Paris – 24 January 1944 in Paris) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tirigan_and_(Tiri.....Lugal_Gutium)_on_the_Victory_Stele_of_Utu-Hengal.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
